WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.497
Cyber incidents create continuity problems when systems, visibility, communications, identity,

00:00:04.497 --> 00:00:08.014
or trust can no longer be relied upon.

00:00:08.014 --> 00:00:10.652
Ransomware may disable HMIs and servers.

00:00:10.652 --> 00:00:12.850
Destructive activity may affect configurations.

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:15.927
Loss of remote access may isolate support,

00:00:15.927 --> 00:00:19.884
while loss of monitoring can make continued operation unsafe.

00:00:19.884 --> 00:00:25.160
Incident response and continuity teams should share one operating picture: affected assets,

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:29.996
process state, safe operating limits, evidence, containment options, and recovery priorities.

00:00:29.996 --> 00:00:31.315
Cybersecurity leads investigation;

00:00:31.315 --> 00:00:35.272
operations and engineering determine physical consequence and safe action.

00:00:35.272 --> 00:00:37.031
Containment choices require care.

00:00:37.031 --> 00:00:42.746
Disconnecting a network path may stop unwanted access but also remove alarms, control,

00:00:42.746 --> 00:00:44.505
redundancy, or historian data.

00:00:44.505 --> 00:00:51.539
Decisions should follow preplanned authority and consider whether a local fallback or controlled shutdown is available.

00:00:51.539 --> 00:00:56.815
A safe shutdown may be the best continuity action when process integrity

00:00:56.815 --> 00:00:59.013
or visibility cannot be trusted.

00:00:59.013 --> 00:01:02.970
Plans should define shutdown prerequisites, communications, staffing, restart conditions,

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:07.806
and the assets that must remain protected while production is stopped.

00:01:07.806 --> 00:01:11.763
Do not rush restoration because business pressure is high.

00:01:11.763 --> 00:01:14.841
Validate backups, images, controller logic, configurations, credentials,

00:01:14.841 --> 00:01:16.599
and dependencies before reconnecting.

00:01:16.599 --> 00:01:20.556
Preserve evidence and determine whether the cause remains present.

00:01:20.556 --> 00:01:24.513
Recovery from a compromised state can reintroduce the incident.

00:01:24.513 --> 00:01:28.030
Communicate clearly with leadership, employees, vendors, customers, regulators,

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:30.229
and public authorities as required.

00:01:30.229 --> 00:01:34.625
After recovery, update process maps, backups, fallback procedures, security controls,

00:01:34.625 --> 00:01:35.505
and exercises.

00:01:35.505 --> 00:01:41.220
Continuity succeeds when the incident is contained without sacrificing safe and trustworthy operations.
